WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
Welcome to First Principles Daily, episode 34 for July 9th, 2026.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.720
Let's run the Magic Wand number and the idiot index on today's subject.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.960
A mile of subway can cost $2 billion in one city and a tenth that in another,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.520
even with similar rock and water tables, because the raw materials and boring hours

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.300
explain almost none of the difference. In high-cost cities, a single mile of new subway

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:35.540
routinely lands between $1 and $3 billion. Comparable tunnels elsewhere finish for two or 300

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:42.160
million. The difference is not geology or tunnel diameter. It sits in the layers of process,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.060
contract structure, and accumulated delay that sit on top of the physical work.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.880
The raw concrete, steel, and machine-time required to bore and line a tunnel

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:59.400
form only a modest slice of that final bill. That is why the gap itself is the clearest signal

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:05.580
that the cost is not set by physics. Urban subway construction follows a pattern that has become

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.720
self-reinforcing. A project begins with years of environmental review and public comment.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.300
It then moves into detailed design that must satisfy dozens of separate agencies.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.640
Utility companies own the rights to move their own pipes and cables.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:27.140
Every relocation, therefore, becomes a separate negotiation that can stretch for months

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:33.620
while crews wait. Labor agreements often require specific crew sizes and overtime rules

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.260
that were written for an earlier era of hand excavation rather than continuous tunnel boring.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.620
Procurement rules in many jurisdictions force the owner to award the lowest bid on a design

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.180
that is already frozen. That leaves no room for the contractor to suggest cheaper methods

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.160
once ground conditions are better understood. Stations add another multiplier.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.020
Instead of repeating a single platform layout, each new station is treated as a signature civic

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:07.800
building with custom architecture, artwork requirements, and bespoke mechanical systems.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.220
Change orders accumulate because the original design cannot anticipate every utility conflict or

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:20.400
groundwater surprise. Each change must be priced and approved through layers of oversight.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:27.200
Financing costs compound during the long schedule, interest and escalation on a multi-year delay

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:34.660
can equal another 20 or 30% of the original budget. In lower cost cities, the same physical tunnel

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.380
is delivered with shorter review cycles, standardized station modules, and contracts that give the

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:46.880
builder more latitude to adjust methods in the field. The high cost path feels normal only because

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.540
every participant has adapted to it. No single actor set out to make the project expensive, yet the

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:59.880
incentive is aligned to keep the process fragmented and slow. One objection sometimes raised is that

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:05.580
dense urban environments simply contain more conflicting infrastructure, so higher costs are

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:11.050
inevitable. Yet the same cities that once built their original subway networks at far lower

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:17.470
multiples of material cost now face the same geology and density. The difference lies in the added

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:23.850
procedural steps rather than in the ground itself. Another common point is that safety and accessibility

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:29.810
standards have risen, and those requirements do add concrete, steel, and systems.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:36.090
Even after accounting for wider platforms, additional emergency exits and modern ventilation

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:42.390
shafts, however, the documented cost spread between otherwise similar projects still exceeds

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:48.310
what those upgrades alone would explain. That again points to the surrounding process layers.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:55.130
A rough magic wand estimate starts with the physical tunnel itself. A typical running tunnel for a

00:03:55.130 --> 00:04:02.010
modern subway is bored at roughly six meters in diameter. The concrete lining might total on the order

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:08.690
of four to six thousand cubic meters per mile. At commodity concrete prices that volume represents

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:15.290
perhaps two hundred thousand dollars of material. Steel reinforcement adds another hundred thousand or so.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:21.210
The tunnel boring machine itself, if purchased outright and amortized across many miles,

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:27.110
contributes machine hour costs measured in the low millions rather than tens of millions when

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:33.930
spread over a full project. Adding power, water, and spoil removal brings the first principles floor

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:39.710
for the board tunnel segment itself into the range of perhaps five to eight million dollars per mile.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.970
Stations and systems raise that floor, but even a fully outfitted station built from commodity

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:52.650
concrete, steel, escalators, and track still sits well below one hundred million dollars when every

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:59.090
element is priced at raw material plus standard fabrication rates. Compare that floor to observed

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:05.790
project costs. In several documented high-cost corridors, the all-in figure reaches or exceeds two

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:11.950
billion dollars per mile. Dividing the finished price by the estimated material and machine floor

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:18.590
produces an idiot index on the order of two hundred or more. That ratio does not mean the concrete

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:25.010
or steel is overpriced. It means the overwhelming majority of the expenditure occurs in activities

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:31.210
that sit outside the physical act of excavation and lining. The largest visible slices

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.650
are extended environmental and permitting timelines that keep capital tied up for years.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:42.430
They also include repeated redesign cycles driven by changing stakeholder demands.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:48.690
They include utility relocation programs that must be negotiated one owner at a time.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:55.650
They include risk premiums embedded in every subcontract because the schedule and scope remain uncertain.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:03.010
Each of those layers adds cost without adding concrete or steel. Because the ratio is so high,

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:08.150
even modest compression of those non-physical steps would move the total price dramatically

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:14.790
closer to the floor. A natural question is whether the station fit out itself rather than the tunnel,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:20.510
accounts for most of the gap. Even granting that platforms, mezzanines, escalators,

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:26.230
signaling and fire life safety systems multiply the material quantity several times over.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:31.870
The arithmetic still leaves the bulk of observed spending in the same non-physical categories.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:38.490
The station concrete volume might reach perhaps 20,000 cubic meters instead of 5,000,

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.750
yet the same commodity pricing keeps that element in the low millions.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:47.550
The documented overruns continue to trace to sequential approvals,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:54.790
redesigns after bids are led and carrying costs during multi-year delays rather than to any sudden

00:06:54.790 --> 00:07:00.970
jump in rebar tonnage or pump prices. A redesign would begin with the station itself

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:05.630
instead of treating every station as a unique architectural commission,

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:11.650
a repeatable module could be developed once and then replicated with only local adjustments

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:18.730
for depth and entrance location. That single change removes repeated design fees, repeated regulatory

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:25.350
reviews, and repeated custom fabrication of platform edges, stairs and mechanical rooms.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:31.630
The next target would be utility coordination. A pre-agreed protocol that lets the tunnel

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:38.270
contractor relocate minor utilities under standard rates. With major transmission lines pre-mapped

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:44.530
and pre-funded by their owners, would collapse months of sequential negotiations into parallel work.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:50.990
Procurement rules could shift from lowest bid on a fixed design to best value contracts that

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:56.990
reward schedule compression and documented cost reductions. That would align incentives so that

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:03.350
the builder profits by finishing early rather than by extending change orders. Policy changes would

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:09.490
also be required. Predictable eminent domain timelines and coordinated environmental review that

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:15.390
runs in parallel with preliminary engineering would shorten the period during which carrying costs

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:22.390
accumulate. None of these moves removes the need for safety standards or public input. They simply

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:28.010
move those requirements earlier and make them apply to a standardized template rather than to each

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:34.670
bespoke project. The genuinely hard constraints remain political. Neighborhoods that oppose new

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:41.250
stations, labor agreements that resist changes to crew rules, and funding mechanisms that reward large

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:47.890
one-off appropriations over steady multi-year programs all play a role. Those frictions are real,

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:53.930
yet they are not laws of physics. They are arrangements that other jurisdictions have already altered

00:08:53.930 --> 00:09:00.330
enough to deliver tunnels at far lower multiples of the same material floor. One objection is that

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:11.230
standardization would reduce the ability to respond to unique site conditions such as varying

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:17.590
adjustable parameters for depth, soil type, and entrance orientation while freezing the interfaces

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:24.710
that repeat. Platform edge height, escalator pit geometry, and ventilation shaft cross sections are

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:30.570
examples of those repeatable interfaces. Only the variable portions would trigger new reviews.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:36.430
Another concern is that shifting procurement toward best value would invite favoritism.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:43.050
Transparent scoring criteria tied to verified schedule and cost outcomes published after each

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:48.610
project can limit that risk while still allowing the builder to propose method changes once the tunnel

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:55.190
face is exposed. One principle is that when the largest cost items are measured in years of review

00:09:55.190 --> 00:10:01.490
and negotiation rather than in tons of concrete, the binding constraint is the number of sequential

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:08.110
hand-offs between separate organizations. Another is that standardization only reduces cost when

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:14.570
it is applied to the interfaces that actually repeat, such as platform geometry and utility connection

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:21.370
details rather than to the parts of the project that truly differ by location. The question is which

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:27.790
city or agency will first publish a single repeatable station module and then measure how many months

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:33.530
and dollars disappear once that template is used on the second and third mile instead of the first.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:40.450
That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost, share it with

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:46.750
someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick and Vancouver. One example

